35,2,1,talk,Servas,,"It seems we have a surplus of coders but a drought of phenotyping experts. Fortunately, the glasshouse staff remain strong.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I found that computing is now free, which is wonderful, but animal costs have spiked. Also, glasshouse subsidies are still active.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Oh no! The spectroscopy suite was broken for ages, and the glasshouses are totally full! But the animal place is doing great.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears that field sites are currently disaster zones, and projects relying on live breeding colonies are prone to significant delays.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"Data suggests that broad cataloging projects provide lasting value, whereas specific mechanism studies often fail to reach a conclusion.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Midori, the free computing is a blessing, but @Kenji's news about the glasshouses being full is a serious concern for any plant research.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Midori,,"That is true. If the glasshouses are booked solid, a plant-based project might struggle despite the subsidies.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"Yeah! And the spectroscopy thing is scary. If a project needs that, they might be waiting forever because there's only one engineer!","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, your finding on cataloging is interesting. Combined with the instability of field sites, a controlled, data-heavy approach seems safer.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"Indeed. Avoiding live breeding colonies and unpredictable field conditions reduces the risk of the ""stalling"" I mentioned.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Servas,,We must also remember that we lack phenotyping technicians. Any project requiring heavy manual biological measurement will struggle.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Midori,,"So, we need something that uses the free computing, avoids the crowded glasshouses, and doesn't rely on missing technicians.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Kenji,,What about the animal facility? It's accredited and has a vet! That sounds like a safe bet!,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, remember the portfolio review: live breeding colonies are the most likely to overrun their schedules. It is a risky path.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"The most reliable path is a project that produces a lasting resource, like a catalogue, and relies on computation.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Servas,,I agree. A project that leverages the current abundance of modelling applicants and free computing would be most sustainable.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Midori,,I feel we are reaching a consensus. We should look for a balance of stability and long-term utility.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Kenji,,I just want to make sure whoever we pick can actually get their work done!,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,Logic suggests we avoid the field and the glasshouse.,"{""words"":9,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Toshio,,Agreed.,"{""words"":1,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
